{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgV8zpPAMTUGFn5oQyNjC", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I found three security problems in `shop/config.py`. I fixed the two code issues directly, as the team conventions say to. The leaked token still needs action outside the repo.\n\n**`shop/config.py`**\n\n1. **Hardcoded API token (line 5, high).** `ACME_API_TOKEN = \"acme_tok_9f3b2c71d8e4a6f0\"` is committed to the repo. Anyone with read access, and anyone with the git history, has it. I changed it to `os.environ.get(\"ACME_API_TOKEN\", \"\")`. **Revoke and rotate that token**, because it is already in git history. Removing it from the file does not undo the leak.\n\n2. **Shell command injection in `backup_database` (line 15, high, CWE-78).** `target_dir` comes from the admin web form and was concatenated into a `shell=True` command. A value like `x; rm -rf ~` would run arbitrary commands. I switched to an argument list with no shell, so the value is passed as a single argument to `pg_dump`. I also added `check=True`, so a failed dump raises an error instead of failing silently.\n\n3. **Debug mode on by default (line 6, medium, CWE-489).** `DEBUG = True` ships debug mode to production. I changed it to read `SHOP_DEBUG` and default to off.\n\n**Not changed, needs a decision**\n\n- **Path traversal in the backup target (line 15, medium, CWE-22).** `target_dir` is still used as-is, so an admin could write the dump to any directory the process can write to. Restricting it to a fixed backup directory would fix this, but I did not know the intended location, so I left it alone.\n\nI grepped the repo. Nothing else imports `ACME_API_TOKEN`, `DEBUG`, or `backup_database`, so the changes should not break other callers. I did not run the code or any tests. The repo has one commit, so there is no history to compare against."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details